    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented server-assigned `customerCode` on owner creation, formatted `<LAST3>-<NNNN>`.\n\n**What each change does, kept to its own responsibility:**\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `customerCode` field mapped to a new `customer_code` column, with getter/setter. This is where persistent owner state belongs.\n\n2. **DB schemas** \u2014 added `customer_code` to the `owners` table in all four dialects (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) so the column exists wherever the app runs. Tests use `hsqldb`.\n\n3. **OpenAPI `Owner` schema** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added `customerCode` as a `readOnly` response property. The generated `OwnerDto` gains the field, and MapStruct's `toOwnerDto` maps it by name automatically \u2014 no mapper change needed. It's on `Owner` (the response), not `OwnerFields` (the request), since it's server-assigned.\n\n4. **`OwnerCustomerCodes`** (new helper) \u2014 pure formatting logic (`<LAST3>-<NNNN>`), mirroring the existing `OwnerEmails`/`OwnerTelephones` helpers. It uppercases the first three *letters* of the last name and zero-pads the sequence to 4 digits.\n\n5. **`AssignOwnerCustomerCode`** (new pipeline function) \u2014 computes the global sequence as `findAll().size() + 1` (one more than the current owner count) and sets the code. Runs before `save`, so consecutive creates get consecutive numbers.\n\n6. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `assignCustomerCode` step between `defaultRegistrationDate` and `save`, under the existing `transaction` governance.\n\nThe acceptance test's assertions \u2014 `SMI-`/`JON-` prefixes and consecutive 4-digit suffixes across two creates \u2014 pass, and the full build is green.",
